WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.713
The third mechanism is lifecycle events.

2
00:00:02.713 --> 00:00:06.802
The HR-driven lifecycle itself,
from attributes to joiner,

3
00:00:06.802 --> 00:00:12.512
mover and leaver design, belongs to the
Federal Modernization Workbook's Book 03.

4
00:00:12.512 --> 00:00:17.136
This chapter covers only the Lifecycle
Workflows tasks that change access,

5
00:00:17.136 --> 00:00:22.274
because those tasks are where mover and
leaver problems turn into access decisions.

6
00:00:22.274 --> 00:00:26.075
A joiner gets the onboarding
package and the department package,

7
00:00:26.075 --> 00:00:28.538
never a direct add to
an application group.

8
00:00:28.538 --> 00:00:31.613
A move is a re-derivation,
not an additional grant:

9
00:00:31.613 --> 00:00:36.764
remove the previous department's packages,
request the new department's package,

10
00:00:36.764 --> 00:00:41.376
and send everything the person still
holds outside packages to a review.

11
00:00:41.376 --> 00:00:45.828
Microsoft's built-in job profile change
template does the first two steps,

12
00:00:45.828 --> 00:00:49.259
and its remove-all-assignments
task defaults to fifteen

13
00:00:49.259 --> 00:00:52.325
days after the trigger, a
grace period for handover.

14
00:00:52.325 --> 00:00:55.375
The program keeps that
grace for standard access

15
00:00:55.375 --> 00:00:58.500
and sets it to zero for Tier
1 and privileged access.

16
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.787
A leaver's workflow cancels pending
requests, removes every package assignment,

17
00:01:03.787 --> 00:01:08.363
removes the user from all groups and
teams, and revokes refresh tokens.

18
00:01:08.363 --> 00:01:11.361
And an inactivity workflow
is the backstop for

19
00:01:11.361 --> 00:01:14.899
accounts whose HR record
never produced a leaver event.

20
00:01:14.899 --> 00:01:19.875
A tenant can have fifty workflows, so
spend them on events, not on applications.

21
00:01:19.875 --> 00:01:23.317
Guests are where unreviewed
access piles up fastest,

22
00:01:23.317 --> 00:01:27.150
because nobody's HR record ends
when a partner project does.

23
00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:28.138
Four layers.

24
00:01:28.138 --> 00:01:29.821
Invite through a package,

25
00:01:29.821 --> 00:01:33.988
so every guest arrives with an
approver, a sponsor and an expiry.

26
00:01:33.988 --> 00:01:38.789
When a governed guest loses their last
assignment, they're blocked and, by default,

27
00:01:38.789 --> 00:01:43.448
removed after thirty days; ungoverned
guests stay in the tenant indefinitely,

28
00:01:43.448 --> 00:01:47.462
so the program marks existing guests
with assignments as governed.

29
00:01:47.462 --> 00:01:49.813
Sponsors review guests quarterly.

30
00:01:49.813 --> 00:01:52.597
And guest lifecycle
policies, still in preview,

31
00:01:52.597 --> 00:01:55.525
will be adopted when
they're generally available.

32
00:01:55.525 --> 00:02:00.488
All of this is licensed by Entra ID
Governance, so plan it for the whole workforce.

33
00:02:00.488 --> 00:02:03.508
Partial licensing produces
a program that governs

34
00:02:03.508 --> 00:02:06.100
some people and leaves
the rest ungoverned.
